WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:14.200
If we look, we can find two types of slider: the first and the second. The difference is that I can move the first one to any value; its step can be one, so I move from

2
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:25.900
one up to 100, say: one, two, three, four, and so on. The second slider moves, say, from one to five, to ten, to fifteen, to twenty, and so on: its steps change by a set value.

3
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:34.750
We call the first one a continuous slider: I move in it by a very small amount. In a discrete slider I set some values that

4
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:43.600
I'll jump between, and I can't get values in between, like we said 1, 5, 10, 15 and so on. Those are the two types of slider.

5
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:52.233
If we start thinking about the guidelines together, we have this example: I enter my weight in an app, say, and I set it

6
00:00:52.233 --> 00:01:00.867
with this slider at the bottom, which of course imitates a real scale; sometimes it moves like this. The designer tried to make it

7
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:09.500
look a bit like a real scale, so they made it this way, and I slide the slider at the bottom to set my weight.

8
00:01:10.048 --> 00:01:20.315
If we think about what the problem or the mistake with this slider could be: we find we move by one tenth here. The amount we move by is very small, and

9
00:01:20.315 --> 00:01:30.581
weight can vary: one person might weigh 60 and another 120. So if I set the default to, say, 80, in every case I'll spend a long time sliding a lot to

10
00:01:30.581 --> 00:01:40.848
reach the exact value I want, which might be 80.4, for example. The slider has a hard time reaching that value, and that isn't what a slider is for in the first place.

11
00:01:40.848 --> 00:01:51.923
A slider is there to choose a value whose exact amount we don't need to know; it can be approximate: we say this value is suitable, or this value is enough. Like

12
00:01:51.923 --> 00:02:02.998
the example here: if we're adjusting a photo's colours, we can make it a bit warm or a bit cold, between warm and cool colours. We move like this and the exact

13
00:02:02.998 --> 00:02:14.073
number doesn't matter to us, but the look does: when we see the look is right now, we stop there. Like the brightness example we talked about: when I reach a brightness

14
00:02:14.073 --> 00:02:25.148
that looks right to me, I stop. And when I reach a volume where I can hear well enough, I stop there. It doesn't have to be a specific number I know.

15
00:02:25.148 --> 00:02:32.898
That's one of the slider's guidelines: I don't always need to reach a specific number, because a slider isn't the best tool for that. I can do it with a stepper,

16
00:02:32.898 --> 00:02:40.648
or by typing the number itself, which is usually the best option in that case. But a slider, as we said, is for a number that doesn't have to be exact.

17
00:02:40.648 --> 00:02:49.881
We can take the second slider guideline from this example: we set a price range, say, or anything with a range in general. In this example

18
00:02:49.881 --> 00:02:59.115
the number the slider reaches in the range, whether the maximum or the minimum, sits under the slider. So as I move like this, my finger starts

19
00:02:59.115 --> 00:03:08.348
to cover the number I reached. This guideline says we need to watch what exactly my finger may cover on the screen while I move the slider.

20
00:03:08.348 --> 00:03:20.798
Of course, this may not apply one hundred percent. Usually, if I press the slider's handle to move it back or forward, I might press it from above on the screen, with my finger or hand

21
00:03:20.798 --> 00:03:33.248
covering part of the screen. Or I press it and move down, so my hand isn't on the slider itself: I move right and left and it moves with me, so my hand isn't covering the screen.

22
00:03:33.248 --> 00:03:45.238
So sometimes, if we don't have enough space, we don't have to put everything related to the slider above it. We can put it below, and make it big enough to be seen.

23
00:03:45.238 --> 00:03:57.228
At the same time, users get used to pressing the handle, moving their hand down and dragging the slider from below rather than from above. You can see this in many examples today.
